\f1\fs22 He had another problem: the Elvi kept breaking into song. At one point they sang words to a tune that, in less degenerate universes, would be recognised as \'93Promised Land\'94. A mere thirty-odd seconds later, after the Jones clones had, with increasingly urgent and threatening gestures, told them to shut it, they resumed singing with a version of Chanta\'92s \'93Unlucky Brain\'94, in which they had worked the name of a squabbling state of America into the title and changed the lyrics to tell of a desperate search in bad weather. Chanta expected there would be a punch-up, but his Elvi seemed to be in complete agreement over the new lyrics. He sighed; the Elvi needed him less and less.\
The Jones clones offered to break the Elvi\'92s necks if they didn\'92t stop singing. It didn\'92t feel like an offer that Chanta could reject.\
In a passage of Elvi silence, Cracker heard a sound like a double crack coming from two directions.\
All of the Jones clones held up a hand: 
\f2\i quiet
\f1\i0 .\
\'93Gunshot,\'94 one of them whispered. As a group, they started heading off in a single direction.\
Chanta couldn\'92t tell if they were heading towards or away from the source of the noise. The Elvi followed the Jones clones. Chanta stubbornly stood his ground for a few seconds, then hurried to catch up once they had all disappeared out of sight.
